### 5. Pyodide Loading Strategy

The 11MB Pyodide download should be **lazy-loaded** (only when user first runs Python):

```javascript
// DON'T load Pyodide on page load — waste of bandwidth
// DO load it on first Python execution

class PyodideRunner {
  constructor() {
    this.pyodide = null;
    this.loading = false;
  }

  async run(code) {
    if (!this.pyodide) {
      // Show loading indicator: "Loading Python runtime (~11MB, first time only)..."
      this.pyodide = await loadPyodide({
        indexURL: 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/pyodide/v0.25.0/full/'
      });
    }
    
    // Redirect stdout to capture console output
    this.pyodide.runPython(`
      import sys, io
      sys.stdout = io.StringIO()
    `);
    
    const result = await this.pyodide.runPythonAsync(code);
    const stdout = this.pyodide.runPython('sys.stdout.getvalue()');
    
    return { success: true, logs: stdout.split('\n'), result };
  }
}
```

### 6. What If User Logs In on a New Device?

```
New Device Login Flow:
1. User logs in → server authenticates
2. App checks: RxDB is empty (new device)
3. Fetch manifest from Atlas (note titles, folders, metadata)
4. Show sidebar immediately (titles only)
5. User clicks a note → fetch full content from Atlas → store in RxDB
6. From now on, that note is local-first on this device too
7. Background: gradually pre-fetch recently edited notes
```

